You can use a graphing calculator to obtain the roots of any polynomial. Casio calculators are user-friendly; Texas calculators are not. Here, I will show how to find the roots first using a Casio calculator, then using a Texas calculator.
You need to follow the following steps to obtain polynomial roots using a Casio fx 9750GII:
\begin{solutionbox} \begin{enumerate}[label=\protect\circled{\color{white}\arabic*}]
\item \begin{bf}Step one:\end{bf}   Press \keystroke{\large Menu} and 8.
 \item \begin{bf}Step two:\end{bf} Press the Poly (F2) menu.\ and select the degree. Now enter the coefficients of the polynomial and press \emph{Solve}.
\item \begin{bf}Step three:\end{bf} At this point, you will have all roots
\end{enumerate}
\end{solutionbox} 
Texas calculators do not provide a default app to calculate polynomial roots and you will have to install the PolySmlt2 external App. You can also use the Polynomial factorization and roots calculator app from 
\href{https://www.alpertron.com.ar/POLFACT.HTM}{www.alpertron.com.ar/POLFACT.HTM}
You simply have to type the polynomial using the symbol \^{} to write powers and press factor to get the root. Make sure you change the sign on the factors obtained in order to obtain the roots.
